{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/a2-explainer-about-the-puyo-puyo-puzzle-game-series-what-the-game-is-how-play-works-in-sim/audio/main-fdfe740c2f4f.mp3","durationSec":292.704,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":2.38,"text":"Puyo is a puzzle game series from Japan."},{"start":2.38,"end":5.2,"text":"The word Puyo is the name of the pieces."},{"start":6.2,"end":9.98,"text":"A Puyo is a round, soft blob, a little like slime or jelly."},{"start":10.82,"end":14.94,"text":"Each Puyo has a color and a small face, so the game looks cute and bright."},{"start":15.7,"end":17.62,"text":"The company Compile made the first games."},{"start":18.44,"end":22.74,"text":"For many years outside Japan, the series had a different name, Puyo Pop."},{"start":25.72,"end":28.58,"text":"Today the name Puyo Puyo is used everywhere."},{"start":29.22,"end":30.62,"text":"The series is a big success."},{"start":31.36,"end":35.94,"text":"Together with the Madō Monogatari games, it has sold more than 10 million copies."},{"start":36.88,"end":42.72,"text":"Many people in Japan know the game from game centers, and many first played it at home on a family console."},{"start":43.88,"end":45.08,"text":"Play is simple to learn."},{"start":46,"end":49.3,"text":"Puyo fall from the top of the screen, usually two at a time."},{"start":50,"end":53.28,"text":"You move them left or right, turn them, and drop them."},{"start":54.06,"end":55.56,"text":"The pieces land and stay."},{"start":56.38,"end":57.56,"text":"Here is the key rule."},{"start":57.56,"end":62.82,"text":"When four or more Puyo of the same color touch each other, they pop and disappear."},{"start":63.4,"end":67.1,"text":"They must touch on a side, up, down, left, or right."},{"start":68.02,"end":70.02,"text":"Touching only at a corner does not count."},{"start":70.7,"end":74.24,"text":"When Puyo pop, the Puyo above them fall down."},{"start":74.88,"end":77.86,"text":"If those falling Puyo make a new group of four, they pop too."},{"start":78.62,"end":79.56,"text":"This is called a chain."},{"start":80.88,"end":83.78,"text":"A long chain sends garbage to the other player."},{"start":84.64,"end":89.76,"text":"Garbage Puyo, called Ojama in Japanese, are gray blobs that do not pop by themselves."},{"start":90.52,"end":94.2,"text":"You win when the other player's board fills up to the top of the third column from the left."},{"start":94.2,"end":98.62,"text":"The first Puyo Puyo game came out in 1991."},{"start":99.48,"end":104.52,"text":"Compile made it for two Japanese home systems, the MSX2 computer and the Famicom Disk System."},{"start":105.1,"end":106.78,"text":"The characters did not start in Puyo."},{"start":107.1,"end":113.16,"text":"They came from an older Compile game series called Madō Monogatari, also written Madou Monogatari."},{"start":113.16,"end":119.18,"text":"Madō Monogatari is a role-playing game where you walk through dark dungeons and fight monsters."},{"start":119.84,"end":123.5,"text":"Its first game came out in 1990, also on the MSX2."},{"start":123.5,"end":127.02,"text":"Arle is the main hero of that RPG world."},{"start":127.68,"end":129.5,"text":"She is a young girl who uses magic."},{"start":130.34,"end":136.58,"text":"In Puyo Puyo, Arle and the monsters from her world became the players you see on the screen."},{"start":137.22,"end":142.4,"text":"So the cute faces on the Puyo and the fighters beside the board all have a story behind them."},{"start":144.1,"end":148.88,"text":"In 1992, Compile worked with Sega to bring Puyo Puyo to arcades."},{"start":149.62,"end":152.74,"text":"The arcade version ran on Sega's System C2 hardware."},{"start":153.36,"end":155.84,"text":"This version was built for two players who fight each other."},{"start":156.46,"end":162.28,"text":"The chain rule from Part 2 now became a weapon, because a big chain drops a lot of garbage on your rival."},{"start":163.04,"end":166.04,"text":"This competitive style was a hit in Japanese game centers."},{"start":166.82,"end":172.66,"text":"Sega also sold the game for its home console, the Mega Drive, and many people in Japan bought it."},{"start":173.42,"end":179.46,"text":"In 1994, Compile released Puyo Puyo 2, called Puyo Puyo Tsū in Japan."},{"start":180.2,"end":181.56,"text":"It added an important new idea."},{"start":181.56,"end":184.76,"text":"When garbage is coming to you, you can make your own chain and cancel some of it."},{"start":185.3,"end":186.44,"text":"Players call this countering."},{"start":187.16,"end":188.94,"text":"It made matches deeper and faster."},{"start":190,"end":192.66,"text":"Compile had money problems in the late 1990s."},{"start":193.42,"end":196.84,"text":"In March 1998, Sega got the rights to Puyo Puyo."},{"start":197.24,"end":199.5,"text":"From that time, Sega owned the series."},{"start":200.44,"end":205.6,"text":"After 2001, a Sega team called Sonic Team started to make the new Puyo Puyo games."},{"start":206.58,"end":209.32,"text":"Sonic Team is famous for the Sonic the Hedgehog games."},{"start":210.14,"end":212.34,"text":"Now the same team made the little blobs too."},{"start":212.96,"end":214.86,"text":"In 2003 they released a new game."},{"start":215.28,"end":220.48,"text":"The game was called Puyo Puyo Fever in Japan and Puyo Pop Fever outside Japan."},{"start":221.2,"end":224.02,"text":"It added a new rule called Fever mode."},{"start":224.7,"end":227.86,"text":"When you counter a lot of garbage, you enter Fever."},{"start":229.06,"end":233.66,"text":"The board fills with ready-made chains, and you pop them fast for a short time."},{"start":234.62,"end":236.82,"text":"The lens from Part 2 still works here."},{"start":237.7,"end":242.16,"text":"Pieces fall, colors match, and a big result goes to the other player."},{"start":243.14,"end":247.12,"text":"The series kept going under Sega, and one later game became very famous."},{"start":247.68,"end":249.72,"text":"It is called Puyo Puyo Tetris."},{"start":250.78,"end":252.66,"text":"Sega released it in Japan in 2014."},{"start":253.4,"end":255.96,"text":"It mixes two big puzzle games in one."},{"start":256.3,"end":262.32,"text":"One player can play Puyo Puyo, and the other player can play Tetris at the same time, and they still fight each other."},{"start":263.08,"end":269.56,"text":"There are also modes where the two games swap during a match, or where Puyo and Tetris blocks fall on the same board."},{"start":270.32,"end":274.3,"text":"This made the game easy to enjoy with a friend who knows only one of the two games."},{"start":275.06,"end":281.26,"text":"Outside Japan, an English version came out in 2017 for the PlayStation 4 and the Nintendo Switch."},{"start":282.08,"end":286.26,"text":"For many players in Europe and America, this was their first Puyo Puyo game."},{"start":286.86,"end":292.06,"text":"The blobs from a 1991 Japanese computer game now sit next to Tetris on a modern console."}],"words":[{"text":"Puyo","start":0,"end":0.46},{"text":"is","start":0.46,"end":0.72},{"text":"a","start":0.72,"end":0.86},{"text":"puzzle","start":0.86,"end":1.1},{"text":"game","start":1.1,"end":1.32},{"text":"series","start":1.32,"end":1.64},{"text":"from","start":1.64,"end":1.96},{"text":"Japan","start":1.96,"end":2.38},{"text":"The","start":2.38,"end":3.16},{"text":"word","start":3.16,"end":3.44},{"text":"Puyo","start":3.44,"end":4.06},{"text":"is","start":4.06,"end":4.26},{"text":"the","start":4.26,"end":4.44},{"text":"name","start":4.44,"end":4.58},{"text":"of","start":4.58,"end":4.74},{"text":"the","start":4.74,"end":4.86},{"text":"pieces","start":4.86,"end":5.2},{"text":"A","start":6.2,"end":6.22},{"text":"Puyo","start":6.22,"end":6.62},{"text":"is","start":6.62,"end":6.76},{"text":"a","start":6.76,"end":6.94},{"text":"round","start":6.94,"end":7.28},{"text":"soft","start":7.54,"end":7.76},{"text":"blob","start":7.76,"end":8.16},{"text":"a","start":8.52,"end":8.62},{"text":"little","start":8.62,"end":8.74},{"text":"like"